WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.371
Your skin has three main layers. The epidermis is the thin outer layer. Below it is the thick dermis. Under the dermis is a layer of fat, also called adipose tissue.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:27.758
The skin is a barrier that keeps pathogens out. Pathogens are microbes that cause disease. The epidermis also holds melanin, a brown pigment. It absorbs ultraviolet light from the sun, so it lowers the risk of skin cancer.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:43.343
Step one: find the layer, then name the structure exactly. In the dermis, each hair grows from a hair follicle, a small pit. Beside it is a sebaceous gland. It makes an oily liquid that keeps the hair and skin soft.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:01:00.071
The sweat gland makes sweat. It flows up a tube to a pore, a tiny hole in the surface. Sweat cools you and excretes, or removes, some water and salts. The dermis also has blood vessels, and receptors that sense touch, pain and temperature.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:13.258
Step two: give a specific job, not a vague one. The fat layer is an insulator: it reduces the loss of heat from the body. Fat is a poor conductor, which means heat moves through it slowly.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:29.679
Step three, for burns: a first degree burn damages only the epidermis. A second degree burn reaches the dermis. A third degree burn also reaches the fat. If skin is burned or cut, pathogens can get through into the body and the blood.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:47.594
Step four: several structures help control body temperature. The hypothalamus, a part of the brain, detects the blood temperature. Then sweat glands, the arterioles in the skin, and the muscles respond. Arterioles are small vessels that carry blood into the skin.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:02:04.039
Now a real exam question. The diagram shows a section of skin, with the epidermis, the dermis and a layer of fat. Part three asks you to describe the role of the layer of fat in keeping body temperature steady. Two marks.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:14.776
Find the layer on the diagram: the fat is at the bottom, under the dermis. Fat is a poor conductor of heat. So point one: the fat layer is an insulator.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:24.533
Point two: it reduces the heat energy lost from the body to the surroundings. So heat is kept in, and the body temperature stays steady.

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:30.625
Here is the full answer. This answer is by olevels.com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:43.846
A common mistake is to write that fat keeps the body warm. That is too vague, and it does not score. Say it insulates, and reduces heat loss. Also, do not call the fat layer muscle.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:54.341
Another mistake is to mix up sweat glands and sebaceous glands. And do not write that melanin blocks the sun. Melanin absorbs ultraviolet light.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:03:13.690
Your turn. A cook burns her arm on a hot pan. The burn goes through the epidermis and into the dermis, but not into the fat. Name this degree of burn. Name two structures in the dermis that could be damaged. Then explain why the burn may become infected. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:29.520
It reaches the dermis, so it is a second degree burn. Structures in the dermis include sweat glands, hair follicles, blood vessels and receptors: any two. The skin barrier is broken, so pathogens can enter her body and blood.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:39.977
So, every time: find the layer, name the structure exactly, and give a specific job. For damage, link broken skin to pathogens entering the body.
